On that special day, Tina Hall, (T.Hall@railroad.robin.de) said...

> > I think you're describing a glottal stop.  It's a sound that's
> > similar to [k] but in deed uttered further back in the mouth
> > and throat, all the way to glottis (=vocal cords).  I think
> > that's present also in German, perhaps in <Verein>, between
> > the syllables Ver- + ein..? It might be much weaker, of course.
> 
> I can't find that sound in 'Verein'.

I suppose he means the nearly unhearable division in the midst of the 
word; we "start again" with speaking the "ein". Similar to beeindrucken, 
geeignet, unerheblich, when there is a very short closure of the 
"Kehlkopf", the larynx, after the first syllable, to indicate that the 
next portion is in fact a word, in its own standing, only supplied with 
an additional prefix. 

This should be working in English, too, like in "unintelligible". 

There are languages that make a much stronger use of the glottal stop, 
especially in South Africa (think of the famous "click song" of Miriam 
Makeba). 


